when $x=-2$ and $y=2.$ These lines divide the $xy$-plane into four regions, as shown below. Since we have a strict inequality, we use dashed lines to divide the plane.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037232/assets/38f5235039b0a5800651759e8da98166451ab72d25e7eaa9772c10b68c3618ff.png)\n\nTo determine the sign of $f(x,y)$ in each region, we pick four test points, one for each region.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037232/assets/a68d4ca3939349cef83c3c84117df44ddacf49d587b1bc8a871918f62d9b6516.png)\n\nNext, we calculate the sign of $f(x,y)$ in each region using a sign table:\n\n|  | Test point | $(x+2)$ | $(2-y)$ | $f(x,y)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Top-left | $(-3,3)$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| Top-Right | $(-1,3)$ | $+$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| Bottom-Left | $(-3,1)$ | $-$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n| Bottom-Right | $(-1,1)$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n\n \n\nFinally, on our diagram, we shade in the solutions to $f(x,y)<0$ using the *last* column of the table above.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037232/assets/1055b7667e7b1c6d27ad7a9d28ec74ae7ed58dac262ae83b5c4028dcee3f9312.png)" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-121275/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1ef881ff-71d4-5268-9cac-32c478dc02a5"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-121275"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-121090"] :question/problem "Suppose that $f(x,y)=x(y+1).$ Which of the following shaded areas gives the solution to the inequality $f(x,y)\\ge 0?$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that $f(x,y)$ equals zero when $x=0$ and $y=-1.$ These lines divide the $xy$-plane into four regions, as shown below. Since we have an \"or equal to\" inequality, we use solid lines to divide the plane.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037232/assets/599d3a551a20e68989e5de517711ae4c7c73255fb4a9d716797cf90dbb32a4ea.png)\n\nTo determine the sign of $f(x,y)$ in each region, we pick four test points, one for each region.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037232/assets/43bb5eac3414291bbd844a3608835a6f7be3e5e0dfcc2ae89426581eccdc87b3.png)\n\nNext, we calculate the sign of $f(x,y)$ in each region using a sign table:\n\n|  | Test point | $x$ | $(y+1)$ | $f(x,y)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Top-Left | $(-1,0)$ | $-$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n| Top-Right | $(1,0)$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| Bottom-Left | $(-1,-2)$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| Bottom-Right | $(1,-2)$ | $+$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n\n \n\nFinally, on our diagram, we shade in the solutions to $f(x,y)\\ge 0$ using the *last* column of the table above.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037232/assets/18757d46e86eb0d771841e7f5fe13be9ad464ccaf66b7190e7a2a7f02428a88e.png)" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-121092"] :question/problem "Suppose that $f(x,y)={x}^{2}(y+1).$ Which of the following shaded areas gives the solution to the inequality $f(x,y)<0?$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that $f(x,y)$ equals zero when $x=0$ and $y=-1.$ These lines divide the $xy$-plane into four regions, as shown below. Since we have a strict inequality, we use dashed lines to divide the plane.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037232/assets/c4aecfe6b9374576ae002e439ff756d41ba29eb87d2f99cf3569dd3332e0128b.png)\n\nTo determine the sign of $f(x,y)$ in each region, we pick four test points, one for each region.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037232/assets/eedbe9fe606042eb34d0067132e5bfccfea6e6528e3eaf7091f0c237a5fe6ca3.png)\n\nNext, we calculate the sign of $f(x,y)$ in each region using a sign table:\n\n|  | Test point | ${x}^{2}$ | $(y+1)$ | $f(x,y)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Top-left | $(-1,0)$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| Top-Right | $(1,0)$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| Bottom-Left | $(-1,-2)$ | $+$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| Bottom-Right | $(1,-2)$ | $+$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n\n \n\nFinally, on our diagram, we shade in the solutions to $f(x,y)<0$ using the *last* column of the table above.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037232/assets/f6c800752188e6dd292d47f7153462077288540dd232f76a03472b2bab3d20c0.png)" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-9412"] :question/problem "Consider the expression $f(x,y) = (3-x)(y+1)^2.$  What are the missing entries in the sign table of $f(x,y)$ shown below?\n\n| $y > -1$ |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $y <               -1$ |  |  |\n|  | $x<   3$ | $x> 3$ |" :question/worked-solution "Notice that $f(x,y)$ equals zero when $x = 3$ and $y = -1.$ These lines divide the $xy$-plane into four regions, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037232/assets/df8519853487a7da738b4b3c477b141e00287c67dcb9eb804cbd385c6f43b611.png)\n\nTo determine the sign of $f(x,y)$ in each region, we pick four test points, one for each region.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037232/assets/4666739c474f85cbf8de7e7ca183206ab7b0bd804816a5504f9c1d97b03956b4.png)\n\nNext, we calculate the sign of $f(x,y)$ in each region using a sign table:\n\n|  | Test point | $(3-x)$ | $(y+1)^2$ | $f(x,y)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Top-left | $(2,0)$ | $\\color{blue}+$ | $\\color{blue}+$ | $\\color{blue}+$ |\n| Top-Right | $(4,0)$ | $\\color{red}-$ | $\\color{blue}+$ | $\\color{red}-$ |\n| Bottom-Left | $(2,-2)$ | $\\color{blue}+$ | $\\color{blue}+$ | $\\color{blue}+$ |\n| Bottom-Right | $(4,-2)$ | $\\color{red}-$ | $\\color{blue}+$ | $\\color{red}-$ |\n\n \n\nUsing the *last* column of the table above, we get the following picture:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037232/assets/e789ce41c696b456e0c9d79ba8ce60aba030e454ab614902ea13c8d9ccd02d8c.png)\n\nFinally, we fill in the required sign table.\n\n| $y > -1$ | $\\color{blue}+$ | $\\color{red}-$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $y <               -1$ | $\\color{blue}+$ | $\\color{red}-$ |\n|  | $x<  3$ | $x> 3$ |" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-9414"] :question/problem "Suppose that $f(x,y) = (x-2)^2(y+1).$ Which of the following shaded areas gives the solution to the inequality $f(x,y) > 0?$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that $f(x,y)$ equals zero when $x=2$ and $y=-1.$ These lines divide the $xy$-plane into four regions, as shown below. Since we have a strict inequality, we use dashed lines to divide the plane.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037232/assets/8e635d2ba2e7ca5721ff8b1dec2055b4ac0c9bcc2eca648848c27190271e92b6.png)\n\nTo determine the sign of $f(x,y)$ in each region, we pick four test points, one for each region.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037232/assets/1ce934096086de33423910710a010400922ea86c66bbfe0b1bfb0d92291ab59e.png)\n\nNext, we calculate the sign of $f(x,y)$ in each region using a sign table:\n\n|  | Test point | $(x-2)^2$ | $(y+1)$ | $f(x,y)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Top-Left | $(1,0)$ | $\\color{blue}+$ | $\\color{blue}+$ | $\\color{blue}+$ |\n| Top-Right | $(3,0)$ | $\\color{blue}+$ | $\\color{blue}+$ | $\\color{blue}+$ |\n| Bottom-Left | $(1,-2)$ | $\\color{blue}+$ | $\\color{red}-$ | $\\color{red}-$ |\n| Bottom-Right | $(3,-2)$ | $\\color{blue}+$ | $\\color{red}-$ | $\\color{red}-$ |\n\n \n\nFinally, on our diagram, we shade in the solutions to $f(x,y) > 0$ using the *last* column of the table above.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037232/assets/70a2ca01e47e91f9d818599886f79b0f00edad0cfd12b080995dfbe6cdf68235.png)" :db/ensure [:question/validate]}]
